{"kind":"propose","text":"CURRENT REVISED DECISION PROPOSAL — RELATIVE FAN-OUT COST REQUIRES A CONSECUTIVE VERSIONED AXIS.\n\nThis replaces retired proposal 85f9060f. It incorporates ratified changes-requested report da685415 without using that stale review chain as structural authority.\n\nDecision. Max-fan-out measures incremental causal-base cost, not total append cost. PREVIEW permits no more than +10 percent median append latency at every measured width through 64 relative to one base. FIRST-PRODUCTION permits no more than +10 percent at every width through 256. Ten percent is the existing scale-envelope convention for the cost added by one dimension, not a value fitted to observed noise. An intermediate nonlinear jump fails even if the endpoint later falls.\n\nRequired measurement lane. The current gitseq.performance/v1 expansion cannot issue this verdict: its one-base depth-1,000 submit_ack case is separated from fan-outs 8, 16, 64 and 256 by 46 other cases and hours of work, and the fan-out axis itself omits width 1. A passing verdict therefore waits for the bounded versioned contract/perflane child request filed with this proposal. That successor lane must expand one explicit fan-out axis block at the contract-selected depth with widths 1, 8, 16, 64 and 256 as consecutive cases, with the contract full-tier five warmups and 100 recorded repetitions for each case. It must keep exact case identity and order reproducible, avoid duplicating or ambiguously naming the ordinary depth-axis case, and preserve the raw samples, exact head, contract and fixture digests, distributions, case order and environment. The cases remain separate distributions; adjacency reduces temporal drift but does not invent sample pairing.\n\nStatistic and reporting. For each width f, report both signed quantities beside the one-base median: the relative increment median(T_f)/median(T_1)-1 and the absolute-unit increment median(T_f)-median(T_1) in milliseconds. Never clamp negative values and never take their magnitude. Report the one-base median itself and every intermediate width. A harness trusted-versus-projected digest mismatch is an error that invalidates the sample; equality in a completed sample is a validity precondition, not discriminating correctness evidence.\n\nLoad. No absolute-difference cancellation is claimed. A ratio cancels only a common multiplicative factor. Making the five case blocks consecutive removes the present structurally hours-distant denominator but cannot remove arbitrary load change even within the block. Disclose load and raw distributions; if another complete consecutive-axis run changes the pass/fail classification, the evidence is inconclusive and must be rerun. The page may not label pass from the present non-consecutive v1 layout.\n\nDenominator hazard. The relative target tightens in milliseconds when fixed one-base append cost falls, but it also loosens when that denominator rises. A regression that inflates fixed and fan-out cost proportionally can keep the ratio unchanged and pass. At observed one-base medians the +10 percent allowance ranges from about 52.8 to 99.4 ms, already larger than the separate 50 ms absolute append budget. Therefore a passing fan-out ratio is never an overall append-latency guarantee. The retained evidence and scale page must show the one-base median and signed millisecond increments beside the ratios, and must keep the fixed one-base 50 ms row as a separate honest miss. Existing request 8add6909 remains parked and its Planner report remains unratified; this proposal neither un-parks it nor authorizes optimization.\n\nAxis meaning. The versioned lane still measures scale dimensions independently at its contract-selected axis depth, currently 1,000. A pass establishes the fan-out dimension at maximum widths 64 and 256, not an end-to-end cross-product guarantee at depth 50,000 or 500,000. Cross-product claims require their own reviewed contract change and evidence.\n\nCurrent non-governing evidence. Workroom medians are +9.7 ms and +1.27 percent at 256 bases. Codex depth-1,000 medians are -7.88 ms and -1.49 percent. Claude depth-1,000 medians are +5.0 ms and +0.50 percent at 256, while depth-10,000 medians are -100.0 ms and -3.81 percent. They corroborate that fixed cost dominates but do not satisfy the required versioned consecutive 100-sample lane.\n\nContracts. This decision changes attribution and evidence ordering only. It cannot be met by deferring or removing actor signing, sequencer admission, verified-ref compare-and-swap, signature or bound verification, complete fold semantics, idempotency, trusted-versus-projected equality, error behavior or atomic publication. No architecture layer contract changes. This proposal governs only after independent support and authorized ratifier adoption."}